ng round was used as the tie-breaker, followed by number of 10s, and then number of 9s in the preceding round.  Matches shot entirely at 70 metres."]] [:tr {:class "records", :style ""} [:th {:style "vertical-align: top;"} "Olympic Record  (Match, 12 arrows)"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/40814"} "114"] " " [:span {:class "label label-primary"} "WR"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/1962"} "Kim Su-Nyeong"] " " [:a {:href "/countries/KOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/KOR.png"}] "KOR"] " /  2 August 1992 / " [:a {:href "/records/event_name/25/Match,%2012%20arrows"} "Details"]]] [:tr {:class "records", :style ""} [:th {:style "vertical-align: top;"} "Olympic Record  (Match, 18 arrows)"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/41175"} "173"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/83170"} "Yun Mi-Jin"] " " [:a {:href "/countries/KOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/KOR.png"}] "KOR"] " / 19 September 2000 / " [:a {:href "/records/event_name/25/Match,%2018%20arrows"} "Details"]]] [:tr {:class "records", :style ""} [:th {:style "vertical-align: top;"} "Olympic Record  (Ranking Round, 72 arrows)"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/40981"} "673"] " " [:span {:class "label label-primary"} "WR"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/2183"} "Lina Herasymenko"] " " [:a {:href "/countries/UKR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/UKR.png"}] "UKR"] " / 28 July 1996 / " [:a {:href "/records/event_name/25/Ranking%20Round,%2072%20arrows"} "Details"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The 2003 World Champion was Korean " [:a {:href ""} "Yun Mi-Jun"] ", also the defending gold medalist. She was in Athens and placed third in the ranking round behind her countrywomen " [:a {:href "/athletes/102820"} "Park Seong-Hyeon"] ", the 2001 World Champion who led the rankings with a world record of 682, and " [:a {:href "/athletes/102817"} "Lee Seong-Jin"] ". By 2004 Korean female archers won virtually every tournament they entered. They had won the Olympic team gold medal from 1988-2000 and the individual gold medals at every Olympics since 1984. The only surprise in Athens was when Yun lost in the quarter-finals and did not make the semis, preventing a Korean medal sweep as they had done in 2000."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The final came down to Park against Lee, with Park winning the gold medal 110-108. Park’s semi-final victory came over surprising British archer " [:a {:href "/athletes/564"} "Alison Williamson"] ", who was competing in her fourth Olympics. Williamson had never won a medal but had always placed in the top 10 in the individual Olympic event. In Athens she came through for the bronze medal by defeating " [:a {:href "/athletes/102840"} "Yuan Shu-Chi"] " of Chinese Taipei."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/102820"} "Park Seong-Hyeon"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/KOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/KOR.png"}] "KOR"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/102817"} "Lee Seong-Jin"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/KOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/KOR.png"}] "KOR"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/564"} "Alison Williamson"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.am